2026-08-30T09:33Z claude note SETUP. padme: Ubuntu 24.04.4, NVIDIA RTX A4000 16 GB (pinned via CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=0) + RTX 3060 12 GB present but unused, driver 580.159.04, CUDA 12.8. Node v22.23.2 installed user-locally under ~/opt/node. Package root at ~/data/zoteus-bench-pkg: @huggingface/transformers 4.2.0, onnxruntime-node 1.24.3, onnxruntime-web 1.26.0-dev.20260416-b7804b056c, onnxruntime-common 1.24.3 — identical versions to the CPU arm, as required. cuDNN was absent (confirmed: the CUDA provider failed to load with `libcudnn.so.9: cannot open shared object file`) and was installed user-locally under ~/opt/cudnn/lib from the nvidia-cudnn-cu12 9.25.1.1 wheel (pip download + unzip, nothing system-wide), per the ticket's authorization. Repo cloned at commit a47f623 (matches this branch's base). Corpus copied from doudou, sha256 949d5af1…eec0ca4 verified identical both ends, 93022 lines.
2026-08-30T09:33Z claude note VRAM CLEARED, author-authorized. A system-level llama-server.service was found holding 14110 MiB (A4000) + 11482 MiB (3060) of VRAM; no passwordless sudo was available to `systemctl stop` it, but the process runs as the same user (User=haduong in the unit), so it was stopped with `kill -TERM` directly — GPU confirmed quiet (89/2 MiB used) before any measurement. It has NOT been restarted; restoring it needs `sudo systemctl start llama-server` (trivial, one line) since I cannot invoke systemctl myself — flagged for the author.
2026-08-30T09:33Z claude note THE LOAD-BEARING CLAIM, observed with ORT verbose logging (`env.logLevel = LogLevel.DEBUG`), not merely a success/failure probe: `device:'auto'` on Linux x64 resolves (`src/backends/onnx.js`) to the execution-provider list `['cuda','webgpu','cpu']`, in that order, and ORT registers all three as fallback providers in one session. For `Xenova/all-MiniLM-L6-v2` at q8, graph partitioning placed 340 of 402 nodes on CUDAExecutionProvider and the remaining 62 (two ORT cost-heuristic CPU-preferred nodes, plus every DynamicQuantizeLinear/MatMulInteger node — q8's quantization ops, for which CUDA registers no kernel) on CPUExecutionProvider; WebGpuExecutionProvider was initialized (a Dawn device context was created, hence the "maxDynamicUniformBuffersPerPipelineLayout" warnings seen on every run) but received ZERO node placements — inert for this model. So on this host `auto` genuinely runs on the GPU, not a silent CPU fallback, with per-node CPU offload for ops CUDA cannot serve.
2026-08-30T09:33Z claude note THAT RESULT DOES NOT GENERALISE. For `granite-97m-multilingual-r2` (registry id) at fp32, `auto` reproducibly CRASHES mid-execution (exit -6/SIGABRT, no output): "Non-zero status code returned while running Sqrt node… All inputs must be tensors on WebGPU buffers" — ORT's partitioner assigned this model's Sqrt node to WebGpuExecutionProvider while feeding it a CPU-resident tensor, a genuine mixed-CUDA/WebGPU-partitioning bug in this ORT build, not a benign fallback. Same model, explicit `device:'cuda'` (no WebGPU registered at all) loads and measures cleanly (load_ms 1940.3, RSS delta 907.4 MB, query_ms_median 3.3). So `auto`'s GPU-bearing provider list is real and the failure IS caught — the process does not silently serve CPU-only or hang — but the failure is a hard crash for at least one candidate, not a graceful same-session fallback. Recorded as a first-class result per the ticket's own instruction.
2026-08-30T09:33Z claude note A SECOND, UNRELATED CRASH CLASS: this ORT/WebGPU-Dawn native binding SIGSEGVs/SIGABRTs on process exit after a successful run — confirmed with `device:'cuda'` alone (no WebGPU registered) and with `device:'auto'` on the working all-MiniLM case alike; the driver's own writeFileSync always completes first (verified: valid JSON/vectors on disk despite the crash). bench/sweep.py's RealExecutor originally treated any nonzero subprocess returncode as failure and sliced only the last 4000 chars of stderr for diagnostics — both wrong on this host: the first discarded good data behind a crash that happens after the work is done, the second truncated away the actual error (repeated WebGPU buffer-limit warnings dominate the tail, burying the one "Sqrt node" line that names a real failure).
2026-08-30T09:33Z claude note HARNESS FIX. `_measure_cost`/`_measure_fidelity` now check the driver's own output file for validity BEFORE consulting returncode (salvaging post-output crashes, annotated with `process_exit_note`), and failure diagnostics are head+tail of stderr, not tail alone. Verified both directions: the salvage path recovers granite-97m's `device:cuda` cost cell that the old code silently binned as failed; the genuine-crash path still reports "failed" with the real Sqrt-node message for granite-97m's `device:auto` cost cell. Also added `--fidelity-rows` (600, to match the CPU arm's discipline — quant_fidelity.mjs's stride sampling is a pure function of corpus length and row count, so two runs against the byte-identical corpus file automatically see the identical 600 rows with no separate coordination needed) and `--vectors-dir` (persists each fidelity cell's raw per-(model,rung) vectors, which the harness previously discarded in a tmp dir — needed for X8, DESIGN §3, which scores one arm's vectors against the other's after both finish). `make check-fast` (173 tests) and ruff stay green.
2026-08-30T09:33Z claude note ACTION 4, probed on the idle RTX 3060 to avoid contending with the A4000 campaign: q4f16 loads cleanly under both `device:'cuda'` (load_ms 7127.3, first download) and `device:'auto'` (load_ms 874.7, cached) for all-MiniLM — unlike fp16, which fails outright on the CPU provider, q4f16 works under every GPU-bearing device value tried. Explicit `device:'webgpu'` also loads and runs cleanly (load_ms 660.4) — the WebGPU path is usable from Node on this host, standalone; the crashes documented above are specific to the mixed-provider partitioning under `auto` for some models, not evidence that WebGPU itself is broken.
2026-08-30T09:33Z claude note ACTION 5. Upstream `huggingface/transformers.js#945` still OPEN, 1 comment, last activity 2024-09-26 — unchanged since the tracker's prior reading. No re-reading of the CPU baselines is triggered.
2026-08-30T09:33Z claude note Cost+fidelity sweep (auto, fp32/fp16/q8/uint8, cost kind + fidelity kind with --fidelity-rows 600 --vectors-dir) launched in background on padme; running as of this entry. Full-corpus wall-clock (action 8) and X8 scoring to follow once the sweep completes and results are pulled to doudou.
--- body ---
## Context

Child 4 of tracker `tickets/0240`, which owns the rationale.

Same corpus, same probes, same driver as the CPU arm — the tracker is explicit
that the two arms share everything but the hardware. What differs is that fp16
is a live rung here and unloadable there, so the arms do not have the same shape
and the report must say so rather than presenting one table.

`gpu-feasibility.json`'s A4000 figures were derived from PyTorch run logs on
another machine: upper bounds by projection, not measurements in the shipped
runtime. This ticket replaces them with real ONNX numbers.

**The load-bearing unexecuted claim.** That `auto` selects a GPU provider is
today read from `src/backends/onnx.js`, not observed. Nobody has run it. That
single observation is the reason this arm exists at all, and it is the first
thing to record.

## Relevant files

- `tickets/0240-...erg` — the tracker
- `tickets/0220-...erg` — why device is `auto` and never a knob; its reading of
  `deviceToExecutionProviders` is the claim under test here
- the harness from `tickets/0262`
- `bench/results/0025-x1-recall/gpu-feasibility.json` — the projections being
  replaced
- `bench/gpu_feasibility.py`

## Actions

1. Reach padme and stand the harness up there. Record the host, its GPU, its
   driver and CUDA version alongside the results.
2. **Observe what `auto` resolves to.** Record the execution provider actually
   selected, from the runtime, not from source.
3. Run the same cells as the CPU arm: fp32, fp16, q8, uint8 per candidate. fp16
   is expected to load here; if it does not, that is a result.
4. Probe the tracker's open engine questions on real hardware: whether `q4f16`
   fails for the same reason as fp16 on CPU or a different one, and whether the
   WebGPU path is usable from Node at all.
5. Record the state of upstream `huggingface/transformers.js#945` at run time.
   If it lands, the meaning of the default changes and every CPU baseline needs
   re-reading.
6. Report the CPU and GPU arms as two shapes, not one merged table.
7. Run X8, cross-provider fidelity (DESIGN §3 owns the rule): score this arm's
   vectors against the CPU arm's same-(model, rung) vectors from 0263, per
   candidate, at every rung both providers load. The existing fidelity scorer
   does the scoring; the fp32-against-itself control applies here too. The
   verdict decides whether the execution provider stays out of the embedder
   key, and the adopt-a-foreign-index ruling in DECISIONS.md waits on it.
8. Measure full-corpus embed wall clock per surviving candidate on this host,
   warm, with repeats. R30's throughput bound (DESIGN §2.8, unpinned) derives
   from the recommended candidate's figure; the pin lands with the gate, in
   one change, never from a projection.

## Test

- The harness's own tests (0262) gate the run.
- The device recorded in each result file is the device selected, and a test
  asserts it is read from the runtime rather than echoed from the request.

## Verification

- [ ] `auto` on padme is **observed** to select a GPU provider, with the provider
      named — the claim is currently unexecuted
- [ ] Each result file names its four engine versions and the host
- [ ] fp16 recorded as loadable or not, with the error if not

## Invariants

- Same corpus, same probes, same driver as the CPU arm
- Warm only; median over repeats with spread
- No projected figure is presented beside a measured one without saying which

## Exit criteria

- [ ] GPU arm complete for every surviving candidate at fp32, fp16, q8, uint8
- [ ] The provider `auto` selected is recorded from observation
- [ ] `gpu-feasibility.json`'s projections are superseded in place, with the
      supersession stated where they are cited
- [ ] X8 reported per candidate at every shared rung, against DESIGN §3's bar
- [ ] Full-corpus embed wall clock measured per candidate, feeding R30's bound
